<GROUND_TRUTH>
We will prove the equivalence in two parts:

1. **Forward Direction (\(a \equiv b \pmod{10}\) implies \(a \equiv b \pmod{2}\) and \(a \equiv b \pmod{5}\)):**
   - Assume \(a \equiv b \pmod{10}\). This means \(10 \mid (a - b)\).
   - Since \(2 \mid 10\) and \(5 \mid 10\), it follows that \(2 \mid (a - b)\) and \(5 \mid (a - b)\).
   - Therefore, \(a \equiv b \pmod{2}\) and \(a \equiv b \pmod{5}\).

2. **Reverse Direction (\(a \equiv b \pmod{2}\) and \(a \equiv b \pmod{5}\) implies \(a \equiv b \pmod{10}\)):**
   - Assume \(a \equiv b \pmod{2}\) and \(a \equiv b \pmod{5}\). This means \(2 \mid (a - b)\) and \(5 \mid (a - b)\).
   - Since 2 and 5 are coprime, their least common multiple is \(10\), so \(10 \mid (a - b)\).
   - Therefore, \(a \equiv b \pmod{10}\).

Thus, we have shown that \(a \equiv b \pmod{10}\) if and only if \(a \equiv b \pmod{2}\) and \(a \equiv b \pmod{5}\). \(\boxed{\text{The statement is true.}}\)
</GROUND_TRUTH>

