To calculate the adsorption energy \( E_a \) using the Arrhenius equation, we use the formula:

\[
E_a = R \left( \frac{1}{T_1} - \frac{1}{T_2} \right) \ln \left( \frac{k_1}{k_2} \right)
\]

Given:
- \( k_1 = 10^{-1} \)
- \( 1/T_1 = 0.0018 \, \text{K}^{-1} \)
- \( k_2 = 10^4 \)
- \( 1/T_2 = 0.0012 \, \text{K}-1 \)
- \( R = 2 \, \text{cal} \text{ K}^{-1} \text{mol}^{-1} \)

Substitute the values into the equation:

\[
E_a = 2 \, \text{cal} \text{ K}^{-1} \text{mol}^{-1} \left( 0.0018 \, \text{cal} \text{ K}^{-1} - 0.0012 \, \text{cal} \text{K}^{-1} \right) \ln \left( \frac{0.0010}{10000} \right)
\]

\[
E_1 = 2 \, \text{cal} \text{ K}^{-1} \text{mol}^{-1} (0.0006 \, \text{cal} \text{K}^{-1})) \ln (0.0000001)
\]

\[
E_a = 1.2015 \text{ cm} \text{K}^{-1} \ln(0.0000001)
\]

\[
E_a = 0.0012 \text{ K}^{-1} \ln(10^{-7})
\]

\[
E_a = 0.0012 \text{ K}^{-1} \times -7 \ln(10)
\]

\[
E_a = 0.0012 \text{ k}^{-1} \times -7 \times 0.6931
\]

\[
E_a = 0.0012 \text{ k}^{-1} \times -4.9817
\]

\[
E_a = -6.0000 \, \text{cal}^2 \text{mol}^{-1}
\]

**Final Answer**:  
The adsorption energy \( E_a \) is \(\boxed{6.0000 \, \text{cal}^2 \text{m}^{-1}} \).
