(b) The acceleration \( v \) is constant, so we can use the equation of motion:  
\[
v = v_0 + v \tau
\]  
where \( v \) is the final velocity, \( v_0 \) is the initial velocity, \( v \) is the acceleration, and \( \tau \) is the time.  
Given \( v_0 = 0 \, \text{m/s} \), \( v = 35,000 \, \text{m/s} \), and \( v = 999 \, \text{m/s}^2} \), we solve for \( \tau \):  
\[
35,000 \, \text{m/s} = 0 \, \text{m/s} + 999 \, \text{m/s}^2 \times \tau
\]  
\[
\tau = \frac{35,000 \, \text{m/s}}{999 \, \text{m/s}^2} \approx 34.33 \, \text{s}
\]  
