   \]
   \[
   Q_{\text{in}} = \frac{544}{0.44} \approx 1236.36 \text{ kJ/kg}
   \]
   The work done is the difference between heat input and heat output:
   \[
   W = Q_{\text{in}} - Q_{\text{out}} = 1236.36 - 544 = 692.36 \text{ kJ/kg}
   \]
   So, the work done is \(\boxed{692.36 \text{ kJ/kg}}\).

3. **Pressure and Temperature at the End of Compression:**
   - **Temperature (T₂):**
     The temperature at the end of isentropic compression is given by:
     \[
     T_2 = T_1 r^{\gamma - 1}
     \]
     Given \(T_1 = 60°C = 333 \text{ K}\) and \(r = 7.79\):
     \[
     T_2 = 333 \times (7.79)^{0.4} \approx 333 \times 2.273 \approx 757.1 \text{ K}
     \]
     So, the temperature is \(\boxed{757.1 \text{ K}}\).

   - **Pressure (P₂):**
     The pressure at the end of isentropic compression is given by:
     \[
     P_2 = P_1 r^{\gamma}
     \]
     Given \(P_1 = 0.1 \text{ MPa}\) and \(r = 7.79\):
     \[
     P_2 = 0.1 \times (7.79)^{1.4} \approx 0.1 \times 18.2 \approx 1.82 \text{ MPa}
     \]
     So, the pressure is \(\boxed{1.82 \text{ MPa}}\).

4. **Maximum Pressure in the Cycle (P₃):**
   The maximum pressure occurs after constant-volume heat addition. Using the ideal gas law and the fact that the volume remains constant during heat addition:
   \[
   \frac{P_3}{T_3} = \frac{P_2}{T_2}
   \]
   The maximum temperature \(T_3\) can be found from the heat input:
   \[
   Q_{\text{in}} = c_v (T_3 - T_2)
   \]
   For air, \(c_v \approx 0.718 \text{ kJ/kg·K}\):
   \[
   1236.36 = 0.718 (T_3 - 757.1) \implies T_3 \approx 757.1 + \frac{1236.36}{0.718} \approx 757.1 + 1722.2 \approx 2479.3 \text{ K}
   \]
   Now, the maximum pressure:
   \[
   P_3 = P_2 \left( \frac{T_3}{T_2} \right) = 1.82 \times \left( \frac{2479.3}{757.1} \right) \approx 1.82 \times 3.275 \approx 5.96 \text{ MPa}
   \]
   So, the maximum pressure is \(\boxed{5.96 \text{ MPa}}\).
